# ARK Infrastructure Discovery

**Status:** Platform product rule v1  
**Companions:** [ark-operator-intent.mdc](ark-operator-intent.mdc) · [ark-station-doctrine.mdc](ark-station-doctrine.mdc) · [ark-authority-vs-configuration.mdc](ark-authority-vs-configuration.mdc)

## Principle

**Infrastructure is discovered, not configured.**

Phones, printers, scanners, payment terminals, and bay tablets **appear** on the network or through a simple physical action (plug in, power on, scan QR). ARK notices. The operator decides **where it belongs** — not how it works.

This is a different installation philosophy than almost every business platform, which starts with *Add device → Configure → Test*.

## Sequence

```text
Infrastructure appears
  ↓
ARK discovers (probe, DHCP, QR, terminal handshake)
  ↓
Operator expresses intent (where / what work)
  ↓
ARK performs implementation (hidden)
  ↓
Station ready
```

Discovery is **authority-adjacent fact** (this MAC checked in, this printer announced itself). Assignment to a station is **operator intent**. Credentials and drivers are **implementation** — never operator steps.

## Examples

| Infrastructure | Discovery signal | Operator decision | ARK implementation (hidden) |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Desk phone** | Provisioning probe | Front Counter vs Service Office | Extension, SIP creds, microbrowser |
| **Receipt printer** | Network announce / USB | Print at Front Counter | Driver, queue, paper profile |
| **Payment terminal** | Terminal pairing API | Take payments at Front Counter | Merchant link, device cert |
| **Bay tablet** | QR / enrollment | Use at Bay 3 | Profile, API token, scope |

The phone should be **persistent** in checking in until someone assigns it. ARK does not care about probe frequency; the operator cares that it appears when ready.

## Relationship to configuration doctrine

| Layer | Question |
| --- | --- |
| **Authority** | This hardware identity exists; it checked in at this time. |
| **Discovery projection** | *A new phone is ready to use.* |
| **Operator intent** | **Front Counter** |
| **Configuration (settings)** | Shop-wide defaults — business hours, receipt format, not per-MAC typing |
| **Implementation** | SIP secret, driver blob, terminal keys — generated, never typed |

Shop behavior belongs in Settings. Hardware identity belongs in authority. **Operators never configure infrastructure by hand** when discovery is available.

## When discovery is not yet built

Bench and support paths may still allow known-identity entry — **master admin / engineer only**, never the default operator loop. The product bar remains: plug in → ARK notices → choose station.

## Anti-patterns

- Operator types MAC addresses, serial numbers, or API keys as the primary path
- "Add printer" wizards before discovery exists on the floor
- Treating discovery as a **Communications** feature — it is platform grammar for any attachable infrastructure
- Skipping the operator **where** decision when multiple stations are plausible

## Floor test

Unbox → plug into Ethernet (or scan QR) → open ARK → choose station → work. No runbook. No glossary.
